    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to refine our reorder logic for some lithium-ion battery cells we get from Shenzhen. We keep hitting stockouts during peak season because our lead times are pretty unpredictable. Can you help me draft a formula or some basic logic for calculating safety stock so we don't run dry while waiting for shipments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Safety stock calculations are crucial for managing unpredictable supply chains, especially with overseas shipments. A common approach involves using the formula: $\\text{Safety Stock} = Z \\times \\sigma_{L} \\times \\sqrt{\\text{Lead Time}}$. Here, $Z$ is the service level factor (how much risk you're willing to take), $\\sigma_{L}$ is the standard deviation of demand during lead time, and $\\text{Lead Time}$ is your average lead time. You'll need to track historical demand variance to accurately calculate $\\sigma_{L}$. This helps ensure you have enough buffer stock to cover unexpected spikes in demand or delays from Shenzhen.",
      "source": "target",
