    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, shrinkage is definitely a factor, but it feels more systemic than just theft or damage. Sometimes we'll have an entire shipment arrive that looks fine on paper, but then a few weeks later, certain components just seem to \"vanish\" from the system or the orders come in much lower than our logistics logs suggest. It feels like there's some kind of chaotic, hidden element in the middle of the transit process that we just can't quantify with a standard percentage. Any advice on how to build a model that accounts for that kind of irregular, almost malicious-feeling disruption?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you're dealing with systemic, chaotic disruptions that defy simple percentage calculations, you're moving beyond standard statistical safety stock into risk modeling. You need to treat this as a \"Black Swan\" event risk, which is best managed by increasing your service level ($Z$ factor) significantly. Instead of relying purely on historical demand variance, you might need to introduce a qualitative risk score for each supplier or route. For instance, if Shenzhen's logistics chain has a known history of these phantom shortages, you should manually apply a higher $Z$ value—perhaps moving from a standard 1.65 for 95% service to 2.33 for 99%—to compensate for that unknown, unpredictable element. This is less about a precise formula and more about applying a conservative, \"fear-based\" buffer.",
      "source": "target",
